text:
Hello, 

 I am investigating a Service Layer Script Engine extension on SAP Business One 10.0 (10.00.330), SP 2605, 64-bit, on Windows. The package is registered and assigned only to a test company. 

 Original A returned HTTP 200 twice with its fixed probe marker. Minimal B contained only the GET entry point and HttpModule response call. Although the management view showed the new version, the first GET in the same authenticated session still returned the old marker. Package identity and file mapping were independently checked. The helper-based causal test was NOT_RUN, so the function-declaration hypothesis remains untested. For the final check, I restored the exact JavaScript bytes from prepared Original A, changing only package version and file name. The management view showed the restored package as OK and assigned only to the test company. One GET returned HTTP 400, SAP error code 513, with the exact message: “Script error: function 'GET' not found”. This reproduces the failure after restoring Original A but does not explain the earlier 200 responses. No shared services were restarted and no business writes were made. 

 Original A declares the documented uppercase GET entry point, calls one response helper, and has top-level requires for ServiceLayerContext.js and HttpModule.js, plus helper declarations for other paths. Minimal B removed those extra helpers and the ServiceLayerContext.js require. A 2018 discussion for 9.3 PL03 reported possible issues with extra function declarations and suggested expression-style declarations: https://community.sap.com/t5/enterprise-resource-planning-q-a/b1-service-layer-script-engine-extensions-development-issues/qaq-p/671231 

 Does SP 2605 still restrict additional function declarations or top-level requires? Are there relevant SAP Notes or patches? How can a package reported as OK still produce “function 'GET' not found” after its exact JavaScript bytes, package identity, and file mapping are checked? Could session behavior, runtime loading, or caching explain the earlier old response, and what supported evidence could distinguish these possibilities without restarting shared services? 

 Thanks. 
 Update: 
 I repeated the comparison with a minimal package containing only the HttpModule response code and uppercase GET entry point. After one native Web Client logout and confirmation, I re-entered the same test company through the existing SSO session. The first GET returned HTTP 200 with the new B2 marker. The immediately following GET returned HTTP 400, SAP error code 513, with the same message: “Script error: function 'GET' not found”. The error response had no marker, so I cannot establish that both requests reached the same script instance or worker. The issue recurred after deploying the minimal package; changing helper declarations alone is not a demonstrated fix. 
 I then re-imported the exact Original A JavaScript bytes under a higher package version. The management view reported the package as OK, but a GET still returned the B2 marker. No shared services were restarted, and no business writes were made. 
 Is there a supported way to verify which script file and runtime context or worker handled each request, and whether a newly imported file was loaded, without restarting shared services? What request-correlated evidence does SAP recommend for diagnosing intermittent GET entry-point resolution? 
 Minimal script used (probe marker anonymized): 
 var http = require('HttpModule.js');

function GET() {
 http.response.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-store');
 http.response.setHeader('Pragma', 'no-cache');
 http.response.setContentType(http.ContentType.APPLICATION_JSON);
 http.response.send(200, JSON.stringify({ probeVersion: 'B2', method: 'GET' }));
}
